# JavaScript Evaluation in MCP-Playwright

This document explains how to properly evaluate JavaScript code in the browser context using the MCP-Playwright integration.

## Evaluation Methods

The [exp_tools.py](mdc:exp_tools.py) module provides `playwright_evaluate` for executing JavaScript in the browser. There are two correct ways to use this method:

### 1. Direct Expressions (No Return)

For simple expressions that don't need explicit return statements:

```javascript
document.title
5 + 5
document.querySelector('.my-element').innerText
```

### 2. Function Format (With Return)

For code that needs to use return statements, always wrap in a function:

```javascript
() => { return document.title }
function() { return document.querySelector('.button').click() }
```

### Common Errors

The most common error is using a return statement outside of a function context:

```javascript
// INCORRECT - Will cause "Illegal return statement" error
return document.title
```

## Automatic Fixing

The system will attempt to automatically wrap scripts with return statements in a function, but it's better to use the correct format from the start.

## Advanced Usage

For more complex scenarios, consider using `playwright_js_locate` or `playwright_multi_strategy_locate` which handle JavaScript evaluation with built-in error handling and fallbacks.
